[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Model Signatures and Input/Output Contracts in TensorFlow"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"When deploying machine learning models in production, establishing a clear contract between the model and its clients is critical. In TensorFlow, this contract is defined by model signatures. A signature specifies the exact inputs a model expects and the outputs it produces during inference."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Signatures in SavedModel"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"TensorFlow uses the SavedModel format to serialize models. Inside a SavedModel, functions are mapped to signatures via signature defs (SignatureDefs). Each signature contains a set of inputs and outputs annotated with tensor names, data types, and shapes."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Defining Signatures with tf.function"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The most robust way to define a custom signature in TensorFlow 2.x is by using tf.function with explicit input specifications. This ensures that downstream serving platforms like TensorFlow Serving can parse the input requirements properly."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"import tensorflow as tf

class CustomModel(tf.Module):
    def __init__(self):
        super(CustomModel, self).__init__()
        self.w = tf.Variable(2.0, dtype=tf.float32)

    @tf.function(
        input_signature=[
            tf.TensorSpec(shape=[None, 1], dtype=tf.float32, name="input_data")
        ]
    )
    def __call__(self, x):
        return {"output_result": x * self.w}

model = CustomModel()"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"Saving and Inspecting Signatures"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once the model is instantiated with an explicit input signature, you can export it using tf.saved_model.save. This creates a directory containing the model assets and the signature definitions."},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"export_path = "./saved_model_dir"
tf.saved_model.save(model, export_path)"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can inspect the saved model's signature definitions from the command line using the saved_model_cli tool to verify the input/output contract before deployment."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"saved_model_cli show --dir ./saved_model_dir --all"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Input/Output Contracts"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always use explicit TensorSpec definitions with descriptive names to prevent client ambiguity."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Design flexible signatures by utilizing dynamic batch dimensions (e.g., None instead of a fixed batch size)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Return dictionaries from your serving functions to guarantee named outputs in multi-output models."}]
